steps.body:
To recurse over a string or vector, do not chop it into smaller copies. Pass the container by `const&` so every frame shares the same data, and pass an index or a `(left, right)` range that shrinks with each call.

```cpp
int sumFrom(const std::vector<int>& v, std::size_t i) {
    if (i == v.size()) {
        return 0;                     // past the end: nothing left
    }
    return v[i] + sumFrom(v, i + 1);
}
std::cout << sumFrom({4, 7, 1}, 0) << '\n';  // 12
```

Passing `v` by value instead would make every call copy the whole vector: O(n) extra memory per call and O(n squared) work overall.

A two-sided check, like a palindrome, moves `left` and `right` toward each other and stops when they meet. Start it with `static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1` so an empty string gives -1 instead of a huge `std::size_t`.

You can also build a result on the way back up. `reverseFrom(s, i + 1) + s[i]` returns the rest reversed, then appends the current character. Each `+` makes a new string, which is fine for short input.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

bool isPalindrome(const std::string& s, int left, int right) {
    if (left >= right) {
        return true;
    }
    if (s[left] != s[right]) {
        return false;
    }
    return isPalindrome(s, left + 1, right - 1);
}

int maxFrom(const std::vector<int>& v, std::size_t i) {
    if (i == v.size() - 1) {
        return v[i];
    }
    return std::max(v[i], maxFrom(v, i + 1));
}

std::string reverseFrom(const std::string& s, std::size_t i) {
    if (i == s.size()) {
        return "";
    }
    return reverseFrom(s, i + 1) + s[i];
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    const std::string a = "racecar";
    const std::string b = "rocket";
    std::cout << a << ' ' << isPalindrome(a, 0, static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1) << '\n';
    std::cout << b << ' ' << isPalindrome(b, 0, static_cast<int>(b.size()) - 1) << '\n';
    const std::vector<int> nums = {3, 9, 2, 7};
    std::cout << "max " << maxFrom(nums, 0) << '\n';
    std::cout << "stressed " << reverseFrom("stressed", 0) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

