Backtracking is DFS over choices. You keep the current partial answer in a `std::vector` passed by `&`, and every step follows one pattern: **choose, explore, un-choose**.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

void printPath(const std::vector<int>& path) {
    std::cout << '{';
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < path.size(); ++i) {
        if (i > 0) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << path[i];
    }
    std::cout << "}\n";
}

void subsets(int start, int n, std::vector<int>& path) {
    printPath(path);
    {{code}}
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> path;
    subsets(1, 3, path);
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (int i = start; i <= n; ++i) {
    path.push_back(i);              // choose
    subsets(i + 1, n, path);        // explore
    path.pop_back();                // un-choose
}
```

The `pop_back` restores `path` exactly as it was, so the next loop iteration starts clean. One shared vector serves every frame, with no copies.

The `start` index means each call only picks numbers larger than the last pick. So each item is used at most once and every path is in increasing order: you get `{1 2}` but never its repeat `{2 1}`. Printing on entry lists every subset. Printing only when `path.size() == k` lists combinations of size k.

Permutations use every item, in any order, so a start index does not work. Instead a `std::vector<bool> used` marks which items are already in the path: set `used[i] = true` before recursing and back to `false` after. Looping `i` from 0 upward makes the output come out in a fixed, sorted order when the input is sorted. For real code, `std::next_permutation` from `<algorithm>` steps through the same order without recursion, as long as you start from sorted input.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void printPath(const std::vector<int>& path) {
    std::cout << '{';
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < path.size(); ++i) {
        if (i > 0) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << path[i];
    }
    std::cout << "}\n";
}

void subsets(int start, int n, std::vector<int>& path) {
    printPath(path);
    for (int i = start; i <= n; ++i) {
        path.push_back(i);
        subsets(i + 1, n, path);
        path.pop_back();
    }
}

void permute(const std::string& letters, std::vector<bool>& used, std::string& current) {
    if (current.size() == letters.size()) {
        std::cout << current << '\n';
        return;
    }
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < letters.size(); ++i) {
        if (used[i]) {
            continue;
        }
        used[i] = true;
        current.push_back(letters[i]);
        permute(letters, used, current);
        current.pop_back();
        used[i] = false;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> path;
    subsets(1, 3, path);
    const std::string letters = "abc";
    std::vector<bool> used(letters.size(), false);
    std::string current = "";
    permute(letters, used, current);
    return 0;
}

